      "summary": "This paper provides faster algorithms for solving the geometric median problem: given $n$ points in $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ compute a point that minimizes the sum of Euclidean distances to the points. The abstract also notes that this is one of the oldest non-trivial problems in computational geometry yet despite an abundance of research the previous fastest algorithms for computing a $(1+ε)$-approximate geometric median were $O(d\\cdot n^{4/3}ε^{-8/3})$ by Chin et. al, $\\tilde{O}(d\\exp{ε^{-4}\\logε^{-1}})$ by Badoiu et. al, $O(nd+\\mathrm{poly}(d,ε^{-1})$ by Feldman and Langberg, and $O((nd)^{O(1)}\\log\\frac{1}ε)$ by Parrilo and Sturmfels and Xue and Ye.",
